<header><p class="st-kicker">colony · China, Tibet and Mongolia</p><h1 class="st-title">Spanish Formosa</h1><p class="st-meta">1626 CE – 1642 CE · capital Santisima Trinidad (Keelung)</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">A small Spanish colony on Taiwan's northern tip, founded from Manila to protect the galleon trade, which the Dutch captured in 1642.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>Alarmed by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dutch">Dutch</a> presence in the south of <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/republic-of-china">Taiwan</a>, which threatened the trade between <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/china">China</a> and Spanish <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/manila">Manila</a>, the Spanish sent an expedition under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/antonio-carreno-de-valdes">Antonio Carreño de Valdés</a> in 1626. It occupied the harbour of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/keelung">Keelung</a>, founding the settlement of Santísima <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/trinidad">Trinidad</a> and Fort <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/san-salvador">San Salvador</a>, and in 1628 built Fort <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/santo-domingo">Santo Domingo</a> at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tamsui">Tamsui</a>.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>The colony was a garrison outpost governed from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/manila">Manila</a>, with Dominican and Franciscan missionaries working among the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/basay">Basay</a> and other indigenous peoples. Hopes of drawing Chinese and Japanese trade away from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dutch">Dutch</a> were disappointed, and the colony was costly and disease-ridden.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/manila">Manila</a>, needing troops against Muslim sultanates in the southern <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/philippines">Philippines</a>, reduced the garrison in 1637 and abandoned <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tamsui">Tamsui</a>. In 1642 a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dutch">Dutch</a> force besieged <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/keelung">Keelung</a>, and the governor <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gonzalo-portillo">Gonzalo Portillo</a> surrendered. Northern <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/republic-of-china">Taiwan</a> passed to <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/dutch-formosa">Dutch Formosa</a>.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/spanish-formosa.jpg" alt="Spanish Formosa" width="320" height="213"><figcaption>Image: Ningyou. · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Flag_of_Cross_of_Burgundy.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1626 CE – 1642 CE</dd><dt>Century</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/17th-century">17th century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>colony</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>colonial</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Santisima Trinidad (Keelung)</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Antonio Carreño de Valdés, Gonzalo Portillo</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Catholicism, indigenous religions</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Spanish, Basay</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/taiwan">Taiwan</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Formosa (Spanish)</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=1634&amp;focus=spanish-formosa" aria-label="Spanish Formosa on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="7908 1253 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Spanish Formosa lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M8228 1509L4910 989M8228 1509L8210 1585"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M4910 989h0M8210 1585h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M8228 1509h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M8228 1509h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks its capital. <a href="/atlas?year=1634&amp;focus=spanish-formosa">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>

